    updLabel: "Last updated: ",
    legalZhAuthoritative: "This translation is provided for convenience. The Chinese version is the authoritative text; in case of any discrepancy, the Chinese version prevails.",
    privTitleTag: "Privacy policy - TinyProductLab",
    privIntro: "“TinyProductLab · Global address generator” produces random records locally in your browser. We require no account, and using the generator never requires you to submit a real name, real address, real payment card or identity-document details.",
    privHData: "What data we process",
    privPData: "The names, addresses, phone numbers, emails, payment cards, employment and account details shown on the page are randomly synthesised content. Generation happens locally in the browser and does not pass through our servers. If you use features such as “save” or “recently generated”, that content is kept in the current browser’s LocalStorage and is never uploaded automatically.",
    privHEmail: "The email field",
    privPEmail: "So that emails pass common form validation, the output uses the domain shapes of mainstream mail providers (for example gmail.com, outlook.com, qq.com, 163.com), while the local part is generated entirely at random. We <b>do not verify whether an account exists</b>, perform no MX / SMTP probing, and never use these addresses to register accounts or to send or receive mail. Please do not treat a randomly generated email as any real person’s contact address.",
    privHCard: "The payment card field",
    privPCard: "The payment card field produces only “surface-level” numbers following the account numbering structure published by card networks (prefix ranges, length, security-code length, display grouping). We <b>neither read nor embed any real BIN / issuer database</b>, display no real issuing bank, account or balance, and offer no card validation, balance lookup or payment verification. The generator deliberately places a digit other than the correct check digit in the last position, so every output <b>fails ordinary card-number (Luhn) validation</b> and cannot be used for an actual charge in any real or test environment.",
    privHHome: "The personal homepage field",
    privPHome: "The “Personal homepage” field always uses a random display address under this site’s <code>/u/</code> path, so it cannot randomly coincide with somebody’s real third-party personal website. These links correspond to no real page, and we neither create nor store content for them; see <a href=\"u/\">about the /u/ address</a>.",
    privHCookie: "Cookies and analytics",
    privPCookie: "By default this site uses no marketing cookies. If basic visit analytics are enabled, generated names, addresses, card numbers or emails are never uploaded as analytics parameters.",
    privHLocal: "Local data",
    privPLocal: "You can use the site’s “clear local data” function to delete saved records, the recently generated list, recently used countries and your interface-language preference. Clearing the browser’s site data removes the same content (including favourites and history).",
    privHThird: "Third-party data sources",
    privPThird: "Countries, administrative divisions, cities and address formats draw on public data sources; card numbering rules come from each network’s published material. For the exact sources, licences and verification dates see the <a href=\"sources.html\">data sources</a> page.",
    privHMap: "Map service",
    privPMap: "When the map area needs to be shown, your browser requests the map images for the current viewport directly from OpenStreetMap (or whichever tile service the site is configured with). Such requests may carry ordinary network connection information (IP, User-Agent, Referer). This site <b>does not</b> send generated names, street addresses, phone numbers, emails or card details as map request parameters. The map shows only the area position of a city / administrative division and performs no geocoding of synthesised street numbers.",
    privHExt: "External links",
    privPExt: "This site may link to public data sources. Once you leave, privacy is governed by the respective third party.",
